large.content:
{{also|largë|larĝe|Large}}
==English==
{{wp|<dab>}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|enm|large}}, from {{der|en|fro|large}}, from {{der|en|la|larga}}, feminine of {{m|la|largus||abundant, plentiful, copious, large, much}}, of uncertain ultimate origin; see there for more. Along with {{m|en|big}}, it mostly displaced {{cog|enm|stour|stoor}} and {{m|enm|muchel}} (the latter surviving in {{m|en|much}} under a different meaning).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/lɑːd͡ʒ/|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|En-uk-large.ogg|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/lɑɹd͡ʒ/|a=GA,CA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-large.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/læɹd͡ʒ/|a=Ottawa Valley}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑː(ɹ)dʒ|s=1}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# Of [[considerable]] or [[relatively]] [[great]] [[size]] or [[extent]].
#: {{ux|en|Russia is a '''large''' country.&emsp;The fruit-fly has '''large''' eyes for its body size.&emsp;He has a '''large''' collection of stamps.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=2|passage=We drove back to the office with some concern on my part at the prospect of so '''large''' a case. Sunning himself on the board steps, I saw for the first time Mr. Farquhar Fenelon Cooke.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Michigan Film Review| page=347| year=1917| passage=Hal Smith, manager of the Ferry Field theatre, Detroit, one of the '''largest''' and prettiest outskirt houses in town, played Metro's "Revelation" for three days last week}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1946|author=Joseph Augustine Cushman|title=The larger fossil Foraminifera of the Panama Canal Zone|passage=Test large, cylindrical, early portion closely coiled and always completely involute, later and '''larger''' portion uncoiled{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Thom Patterson|title=Stats reveal how many Americans volunteer and where|url=https://www.cnn.com/2018/07/19/us/volunteering-statistics-cfc|date=2018-07-19|passage=The '''largest''' age group for volunteers was 35-44, the CNCS survey said, and volunteers were most likely to be parents with children under 18.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250812222802/https://www.cnn.com/2018/07/19/us/volunteering-statistics-cfc|archivedate=12 August 2025}}
# {{lb|en|especially|clothing|food|or|drink}} That is large {{gl|the manufactured size}}.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Abundant; ample.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=5|passage=We have yet '''large''' day.|year=1873}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1895|title=Kindergarten Review|section=Finger Play|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Kindergarten_Review/n4A-AQAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=thumbkin+tall+man&pg=PA75&printsec=frontcover|author=Margaret Naomi MacArthur|passage=He prefers teachers with '''large''' experience, but often has positions for beginners who have had a thorough preparation.|page=75}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Full in statement; diffuse; profuse.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1485|author=w:Thomas Malory|title={{w|Le Morte Darthur}} Book XX, Chapter xj, [https://quod.lib.umich.edu/c/cme/MaloryWks2/1:22.11?rgn=div2;view=fulltext leaf 408r-v]|text=''And where hit please yow to saye that I haue holden my lady youre Quene yeres and wynters / vnto that I shal euer make a '''large''' ansuer''<br>"And where it please you to say that I have holden my lady your queen years and winters, unto that I shall ever make a '''large''' answer"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1711|author=w:Henry Felton|title=Dissertation on Reading the Classics
|passage=I might be very '''large''' upon the importance and advantages of education.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Free; unencumbered.
#* {{RQ:Tasso Fairfax Godfrey of Bulloigne
|passage=Of burdens all he set the Paynims '''large'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Unrestrained by decorum; said of language.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Much Ado About Nothing|act=II|scene=iii|passage=Some '''large''' jests he will make.}}
# {{senseid|en|nautical}} {{lb|en|nautical}} Crossing the line of a ship's course in a favorable direction; said of the wind when it is [[abeam]], or between the beam and the quarter.

