====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er,more}}

# {{lb|en|Ireland|UK|NZ (dated)}} High in price; [[expensive]].
#: {{syn|en|costly|lickpenny|spenny|Thesaurus:expensive}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''dearer''' the jewel, the greater the love expressed.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|IV|1|text=There's more depends on this than on the value.<br>The '''dearest''' ring in Venice will I give you,<br>And find it out by proclamation:<br>Only for this, I pray you, pardon me.}}
#* '''1902''', ''Briquettes as Fuel in Foreign Countries'' (report of the United States Bureau of Foreign Commerce):
#*: This water is sold for 50 cents per ton, which is not '''dear''' under the circumstances.
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1966|author=The Beatles|title=w:When I'm Sixty-Four
|passage=Every summer we can rent a cottage in the Isle of Wight, if it's not too '''dear'''.}}
# [[love|Loved]]; [[lovable]].
#: {{syn|en|lief|qq=archaic|lovely|precious|Thesaurus:beloved}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=62|passage="Yes, children '''dear''', wait a bit till it turns itself," she answered - she ought to have said "till I turn it"[.]}}
#* {{RQ:Ferguson Zollenstein|IV
|passage=So this was my future home, I thought!{{...}}Backed by towering hills, the but faintly discernible purple line of the French boundary off to the southwest, a sky of palest Gobelin flecked with fat, fleecy little clouds, it in truth looked a '''dear''' little city; the city of one's dreams.}}
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Age of Consent|year=1962|page=129|passage="We shall have to put up with whitebait. And, of course, a '''dear''' little chicken with peas and roast potatoes."}}
# Lovely; [[kind]].
#: {{syn|en|benevolent|benign|nice|Thesaurus:kindly}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Lost World|passage="I've never met the kind of man I mean." "Tell me about him. What does he look like?" "Oh, he might look very much like you." "How '''dear''' of you to say that!"}}
# Loving, [[affectionate]], [[heartfelt]]
#: {{syn|en|cordial|fond|warm-hearted|Thesaurus:affectionate}}
#: {{ux|en|Such '''dear''' embrace tenderly comforts even in this '''dear''' sorrow.}}
# [[precious|Precious]] to or greatly [[value]]d by someone.
#: {{syn|en|cherished|treasured|without price|Thesaurus:valuable}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''dearer''' the giver, the '''dearer''' the trinket he brings!}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XXXVII|58|{{...}} '''dear''' as sacramental wine<br>To dying lips is all he said {{...}}.}}
# {{n-g|A formal way to start (possibly after ''my'') addressing somebody at the beginning of a letter, memo etc.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Dear''' Sir/Madam/Miss, please notice our offices will be closed during the following bank holidays:{{nb...}}.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Dear'''est Joseph/son:...}}
# {{n-g|A formal way to start (often after ''my'') addressing somebody one likes or regards kindly.}}
#: {{ux|en|My '''dear''' friend, I feel better as soon as you come sit beside my sickbed!}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=7|passage=“A very welcome, kind, useful present, that means to the parish. By the way, Hopkins, let this go no further. We don't want the tale running round that a rich person has arrived. Churchill, my '''dear''' fellow, we have such greedy sharks, and wolves in lamb's clothing.{{nb...}}”}}
# {{n-g|An ironic way to start (often after ''my'') addressing an inferior or someone one dislikes.}}
#: {{ux|en|My '''dear''' man, you ought to think twice about who you're trying to blackmail.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[noble|Noble]].
#: {{syn|en|great|respected|worthy}}

