# {{n-g|Also used in various other idiomatic combinations: '''[[at a pinch]]''', '''[[at all]]''', '''[[at fault]]''', '''[[at pains]]''', '''[[at risk]]''', '''[[at that]]''', etc.; see the individual entries.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* ''At'' with an animated object can have a more hostile connotation than other prepositions:
** ''He threw the ball to me'' &mdash; (so I could catch it).
** ''He threw the ball at me'' &mdash; (trying to hit me with it).
** ''He talked to her'' &mdash; (conversationally).
** ''He shouted at her'' &mdash; (aggressively).
* ''At'' with the name of a city or town has a dated, formal, or legalistic character: ''Done '''at''' Rome this tenth day of...''. Today the most common, neutral all-purpose preposition used with cities is {{m|en|in}}: ''I was '''in''' Rome last week''; ''The Louvre is '''in''' Paris.''

