===Etymology 1===

====Article====
{{head|frr|article}} {{lb|frr|Föhr-Amrum}}

# [[the]] {{q|feminine and neuter definite article, reduced form}}
#: {{coordinate terms|frr|det|q=full form}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The article form {{m|frr||at}} can be used with all feminine and neuter nouns. However, some original feminines may still take the older form {{m|frr|a}} (otherwise now restricted to masculines). This group of feminines consists of a limited number of everyday words, including (but not restricted to) those for relatives, body parts and items of clothing. The article {{m|frr||a}} is used with these especially when the sense is either [[possessive]] or [[generic]] (i.e. referring to a thing as such without regard to any particular item). For example: {{m|frr||Hi hee '''a''' hun breegen.|t=He broke the [i.e. his] hand.}} {{m|fr||At wiar so stal üüs uun '''a''' sark.|t=It was so silent as at the [i.e. any] church.}}

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{alt|frr|e|et||Mooring}}

=====See also=====
{{frr-Foehr-articles}}

===Etymology 2===

====Pronoun====
{{head|frr|pronoun}} {{lb|frr|Föhr-Amrum}}

# {{form of|frr|Reduced form|hat|t=it|pos=subject}}
# {{form of|frr|Reduced form|ham|t=it|pos=object}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The form {{m|frr||at}} is always unstressed, but not necessarily [[enclitic]] like other reduced forms.

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{alt|frr|'t||enclitic}}
* {{alt|frr|et||Mooring|Sylt}}

=====See also=====
{{frr-personal pronouns/Foehr}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|frr|ofs|*jit}}, from {{inh|frr|gmw-pro|*jit|t=you two}}. Regarding the Sylt Frisian forms {{m|frr||at|t=the two of you}} versus {{m|frr|jat|t=the two of them}}, it is clear that {{m|frr||jat}} became at some point associated with {{m|frr|[[ja]], [[jam]], [[jaar]]|t=they, them, their}}. For a while {{m|frr||jat}} must have had both senses, which was facilitated by the general overlap between second-person and third-person plural forms in North Frisian; compare {{m|frr|jam}}, which means “them” on Sylt, “you [plural]” on Föhr and Amrum, and both of these in Mooring Frisian. The form {{m|frr||at}} may have been originally an enclitic byform of {{m|frr||jat}}, or may have been backformed later to reintroduce a distinction between second and third person.

====Pronoun====
{{head|frr|pronoun}} {{lb|frr|Sylt|dated}}

# [[you]] [[two]], the two of you {{gloss|second-person dual personal pronoun}}

