===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:different|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|different}}, from {{der|en|fro|different}}, from {{uder|en|la|differēns}}, present active participle of {{m|la|differō||to differ}}. See also {{m|en|differ}}. Mostly {{lg|displaced|supplanted}} native {{noncog|ang|ungelic}} in common usage, which lives on as {{m|en|unalike}} in {{w|Modern English}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪf.ə.ɹənt/|/ˈdɪf.ɹənt/|a=RP,AU,ZA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪf.ɚ.ənt/|/ˈdɪf.ɹənt/|a=GA,CA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-different.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{hyph|en|dif|fer|ent}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{sid|en|not the same}} Not the [[same]]; exhibiting a [[difference]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:different}}
#: {{ant|en|undifferent|Thesaurus:similar}}
#: {{ux|en|— Is your grandpa any better?<br/>— No '''different'''. Still ill.}}
#: {{ux|en|The reality is far '''different''': mine is far superior to yours.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=At Elizabeth-Jane mentioning how greatly Lucetta had been jeopardized, he exhibited an agitation '''different''' in kind no less than in intensity from any she had seen in him before.|title=[[S:The Mayor of Casterbridge/Chapter 29|The Mayor of Casterbridge]]|author=w:Thomas Hardy|year=1886}}
#* '''1915''', Edward Knobel, ''[https://ia600201.us.archive.org/35/items/cu31924012300491/cu31924012300491.pdf Ptolemy's Catalogue of Stars – A Revision of the Almagest]'', page 14 (showing that "to" was used by an Englishman in 1915)
#*: One interesting feature was remarked by Dr. Peters, viz.: that the instrument used for the longitudes of the original catalogue was graduated '''differently''' to that used for the latitudes.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author=William S. Burroughs|title={{w|The Wild Boys (novel)|The Wild Boys: A Book of the Dead}}|page=6
|passage=Enter the American tourist. He thinks of himself as a good guy but when he looks in the mirror to shave this good guy he has to admit that "well, other people are '''different''' from me and I don't really like them." This makes him feel guilty toward other people.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-07-19|author=Ian Sample|title=Irregular bedtimes may affect children's brains|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly|volume=189|issue=6|page=34|url=https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2013/jul/08/irregular-bedtimes-affect-childrens-brains
|passage=Irregular bedtimes may disrupt healthy brain development in young children, according to a study of intelligence and sleeping habits. ¶ Going to bed at a '''different''' time each night affected girls more than boys, but both fared worse on mental tasks than children who had a set bedtime, researchers found.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261018015540/https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2013/jul/08/irregular-bedtimes-affect-childrens-brains|archivedate=18 October 2026}}
# {{sid|en|various}} [[various|Various]], [[assorted]], [[diverse]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:assorted}}
#: {{ant|en|undifferent|homogeneous}}
#: {{ux|en|My daughter told me about all the '''different''' animals she saw at the zoo.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Delbert S. Elliott; et al|title=Good Kids from Bad Neighborhoods: Successful Development in Social Context|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=Ltx9_9NU4v0C|page=19|publisher=Cambridge University Press|isbn=9780521863575
|passage=In any case, poor black respondents living in high-poverty neighborhoods are most likely to view their neighborhood as a single block or block group and to use this definition consistently when asked about '''different''' neighborhood characteristics and activities.}}
# [[distinct|Distinct]], [[separate]]; {{n-g|used for emphasis after numbers and other determiners of quantity}}.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:separate}}
#: {{ant|en|undifferent|Thesaurus:equal}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=May-June|author=Charles T. Ambrose|title=Alzheimer’s Disease|magazine=w:American Scientist|volume=101|issue=3|page=200|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/3/alzheimerrsquos-disease-the-great-morbidity-of-the-21st-century|passage=Similar studies of rats have employed four '''different''' intracranial resorbable, slow sustained release systems—surgical foam, a thermal gel depot, a microcapsule or biodegradable polymer beads.|accessdate=23 June 2013|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130424010003/http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/3/alzheimerrsquos-disease-the-great-morbidity-of-the-21st-century|archivedate=24 April 2013}}
#: {{ux|en|Several '''different''' scientists all reached this conclusion at about the same time.}}
# Unlike most others; [[unusual]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:strange}}
#: {{ant|en|undifferent|Thesaurus:normal}}
#: {{ux|en|What do you think of my new hairdo? - Well, it's '''different'''.}}

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|not the same}} Depending on dialect, time period, and register, the adjective {{m|en||different|not the same}} may be construed with one of the prepositions {{m|en|from}}, {{m|en|to}}, and {{m|en|than}}, or with the subordinating conjunction {{m|en|than}}. Of these, {{m|en||from}} is more common in formal registers than in informal ones; {{m|en||than}} is more common in the US; and {{m|en||to}} is more common in the UK, in Australia{{,}} and in New Zealand. Style guides often advocate {{m|en||different from}}, by analogy with {{m|en|differ|differ from}} rather than {{m|en||*differ than}} or {{m|en||*differ to}}, and {{lg|proscribe}} {{m|en||different than}} and {{m|en||different to}}.

