===Etymology 1===
{{ety|en|:from|en:quit<id:leave>|:influence|xno:quite|tree=1}}
A development of {{m|en|quit}}, influence by {{uder|en|xno|quite}}. {{doublet|en|coy|quit|quiet|quietus}}. For an analogous semantic development from the same root, compare {{cog|hy|շատ}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|kwīt|a=RP,GA}}, {{IPA|en|/kwaɪ̯t/|[ˈkʰw̥aɪ̯t]}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-quite.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪt|s=1}}

====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

# To the [[greatest]] extent or degree; [[completely]], [[entirely]].
#: {{syn|en|very|absolutely|fully|thoroughly|totally|utterly|Thesaurus:completely}}
## {{n-g|With verbs, especially past participles.}} {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=VIII|stanza=49|page=118|passage=Thus when they had the witch diſrobed '''quight''', / And all her filthy feature open ſhowne, / They let her goe at will, and wander waies vnknowne.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=4 October 2005|author=Adrian Searle|journal=The Guardian
|passage=Nobuyoshi Araki has been called a monster, a pornographer and a genius—and the photographer '''quite''' agrees.}}
## {{n-g|With prepositional phrases and spatial adverbs.}} {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1891|author=w:Thomas Nelson Page|title=On Newfound River
|passage=Margaret passed '''quite''' through the pines, and reached the opening beyond which was what was once the yard, but was now, except for a strip of flower-border and turf which showed care, simply a tangle of bushes and briars.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=30 October 2010|author=Joanna Briscoe|journal=The Guardian
|passage=Religion and parochial etiquette are probed to reveal unhealthy, and sometimes shockingly violent, internal desires '''quite''' at odds with the surface life of a town in which tolerance is preached.}}
## {{n-g|With predicative adjectives.}} {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Burroughs Son of Tarzan|chapter=V|pages=66–67|pageref=67|passage=''El adrea'' was '''quite''' dead. No more will he slink silently upon his unsuspecting prey.}}
##* {{RQ:C. S. Lewis Lion|passage=It was almost '''quite''' dark in there and she kept her arms stretched out in front of her so as not to bump her face into the back of the wardrobe.}}
##* {{RQ:Schuster Hepaticae|volume=V|page=5|text=In Lejeuneaceae vegetative branches normally originate from the basiscopic basal portion of a lateral segment half, as in the Radulaceae, and the associated leaves, therefore, are '''quite''' unmodified.}}
## {{n-g|With attributive adjectives, following an (especially indefinite) [[article]]; chiefly as expressing contrast, difference etc.}} {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=w:Richard Dawkins|title=A Devil's Chaplain
|passage=When I warned him that his words might be offensive to identical twins, he said that identical twins were a '''quite''' different case.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=18 September 2011|author=Peter Preston|journal=The Observer
|passage=Create a new, '''quite''' separate, private company – say Murdoch Newspaper Holdings – and give it all, or most of, the papers that News Corp owns.}}
## {{n-g|Preceding nouns introduced by the indefinite article. Chiefly in negative constructions.}} {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Boswell Johnson|year=1763|page=227|passage=I ventured to hint that he was not '''quite''' a fair judge, as [[w:Charles Churchill (satirist)|[Charles] Churchill]] had attacked him violently.}}
##* {{RQ:Galsworthy In Chancery|chapter=Timothy Stays the Rot|page=212|passage=And with a prolonged sound, not '''quite''' a sniff and not '''quite''' a snort, he trod on Euphemia's toe, and went out, leaving a sensation and a faint scent of barley−sugar behind him.}}
## {{n-g|With adverbs of [[manner]].}} {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=John F. Schmutz|title=The Battle of the Crater: A complete history
|passage=However, the proceedings were '''quite''' carefully orchestrated to produce what seemed to be a predetermined outcome.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=18 October 2011|author=Bob Burgess|journal=The Guardian
|passage=Higher education institutions in the UK are, '''quite''' rightly, largely autonomous.}}
# In a fully justified sense; [[truly]], [[perfectly]], [[actually]].
## {{n-g|Coming before the indefinite article and an attributive adjective. (Now largely merged with moderative senses, below.)}} {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1898|author=w:Charles Garvice|title=Nell of Shorne Mills
|passage="My little plot has been rather successful, after all, hasn't it?" "'''Quite''' a perfect success," said Drake.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=7 February 2001|author=Paul Brown|journal=The Guardian
|passage=While the government claims to lead the world with its plans to reduce carbon dioxide emissions, the figures tell '''quite''' a different story.}}
## {{n-g|With plain adjectives, past participles, and adverbs.}} {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Ferguson Zollenstein|chapter=IV|passage=“My Continental prominence is improving,” I commented dryly. / Von Lindowe cut at a furze bush with his silver-mounted rattan. / “'''Quite''' so,” he said as dryly, his hand at his mustache. “I may say if your intentions were known your life would not be worth a curse.”}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=5 November 2010|author=Dave Hill|journal=The Guardian
|passage=London Underground is '''quite''' unique in how many front line staff it has, as anyone who has travelled on the Paris Metro or New York Subway will testify.}}
## {{n-g|Coming before the definite article and an attributive superlative.}} {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Saki Reginald in Russia|story=The Soul of Laploshka|page=66|passage=Laploshka was one of the meanest men I have ever met, and '''quite''' one of the most entertaining.}}
##* {{RQ:Time|date=8 October 1923|title=The New Pictures
|passage=Scaramouche has already been greeted as the finest French Revolution yet brought to the screen-and even if you are a little weary of seeing a strongly American band of sans-culottes demolish a pasteboard Paris, you should not miss Scaramouche, for it is '''quite''' the best thing Rex Ingram has done since The Four Horsemen.}}
## {{n-g|Before a noun preceded by an indefinite article; now often with ironic implications that the noun in question is particularly noteworthy or remarkable.}} {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##* '''1830''', Senate debate, 15 April:
##*: To debauch the Indians with rum and cheat them of their land was '''quite''' a Government affair, and not at all criminal; but to use rum to cheat them of their peltry, was an abomination in the sight of the law.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Gilbert Morris|title=The Crossing
|passage=“Looks like you and Clay had '''quite''' a party,” she said with a glimmer in her dark blue eyes.}}
## {{n-g|Before a noun preceded by the definite article.}} {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##: ''It's not '''quite''' the colour I am looking for''.
##* {{RQ:Trollope Eustace Diamonds|chapter=The Aspirations of Mr. Emilius|page=288|column=1|passage=It is '''quite''' the proper thing for a lady to be on intimate, and even on affectionate, terms with her favourite clergyman, and Lizzie certainly had intercourse with no clergyman who was a greater favourite with her than Mr. Emilius.}}
##* {{RQ:Time|date=6 February 2006|author=Sherman Alexie|title=When the story stolen is your own
|passage=His memoir features a child named Tommy Nothing Fancy who suffers from and dies of a seizure disorder. '''Quite''' the coincidence, don't you think?}}
## {{lb|en|now|rare}} {{n-g|With prepositional or adverbial phrases.}} {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
# {{senseid|en|somewhat}} To a [[moderate]] extent or degree; [[somewhat]], [[rather]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#: {{usex|en|Mind your shoes, the basement is '''quite''' wet.}}
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:moderately}}
#{{rfdef|en}}
#*{{quote-web|en|date=26 September 2026|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cqx2zz6d5716o|author=Phil McNulty|work=BBCSport|title=England embrace the chaos - but control also needed to win big prizes|text='''Quite''' how England reached the 36th minute only one goal down was a mystery to everyone inside Wembley, Marc Guehi's inexplicable turn into trouble gifting Lamine Yamal an opener inside two minutes.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260930152013/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cqx2zz6d5716o|archivedate=30 September 2026}}

=====Usage notes=====
* This is a ''non-descriptive qualifier'', similar to [[fairly]] and [[rather]] and [[somewhat]]. Used where a plain adjective needs to be modified, but cannot be qualified. When spoken, the meaning can vary with the tone of voice and stress. ''He was '''quite''' big'' can mean anything from "not exactly small" to "almost huge".

