===Etymology 2===
{{ety|en|id=verb|:inh|enm:helpen}}
From {{inh|en|enm|helpen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|helpan|t=to help, aid, assist, benefit, relieve, cure}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*helpan}}, {{inh|en|gem-pro|*helpaną|t=to help}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ḱelb-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*ḱelp-|t=to help}}.

Cognate with {{cog|frr|halep}}, {{m|frr|heelpe}}, {{m|frr|help|t=to help}}, {{cog|stq| hälpe|t=to help}}, {{cog|fy|helpe|t=to help}}, {{cog|cim|hölfan|t=to help}}, {{cog|nl|helpen|t=to help}}, {{cog|de|helfen|t=to help}}, {{cog|nds|helpen}}, {{m|nds|hölpen}}, {{m|nds|hülpen|t=to help}}, {{cog|lb|hëllefen|t=to help}}, {{cog|yi|העלפֿן|t=to help}}, {{cog|da|hjælpe|t=to help}}, {{cog|fo,is|hjálpa|t=to help}}, {{cog|nb|hjelpe|t=to help}}, {{cog|nn|hjelpa}}, {{m|cog|hjelpe|t=to help}}, {{cog|sv|hjälpa|t=to help}}, {{cog|got|𐌷𐌹𐌻𐍀𐌰𐌽|t=to help}}, {{cog|lt|šelpti|t=to help, support}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|+|+|+,holp<l:archaic>|+,holpen<l:archaic>}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To provide [[assistance]] to (someone or something).
#: {{syn|en|see through}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''helped''' his grandfather cook breakfast.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=76
|title=Snakes and ladders
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/books-and-arts/21579801-making-sense-statistics-riddle-our-days-snakes-and-ladders
|passage=Risk is everywhere.{{...}}For each one there is a frighteningly precise measurement of just how likely it is to jump from the shadows and get you. “The Norm Chronicles”{{...}}aims to '''help''' data-phobes find their way through this blizzard of risks.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To assist (a person) in getting something, especially food or drink at table; used with ''to''.
#: {{ux|en|It is polite to '''help''' your guests to food before serving yourself.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Help''' yourself to whatever's in the fridge.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To contribute in some way to.
#: {{ux|en|The white paint on the walls '''helps''' make the room look brighter.}}
#: {{ux|en|If you want to get a job, it '''helps''' to have some prior experience.}}
#: {{ux|en|I've already taken three pills, but they don't seem to '''help''' my headache.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To provide assistance.
#: {{ux|en|She was struggling with the groceries, so I offered to '''help'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Please, '''help'''!}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=19|passage=As soon as Julia returned with a constable, Timothy, who was on the point of exhaustion, prepared to give over to him gratefully. The newcomer turned out to be a powerful youngster, fully trained and eager to '''help''', and he stripped off his tunic at once.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-29|volume=407|issue=8842|page=72–3
|title=A punch in the gut
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/international/science-and-technology/21580133-how-microbes-promote-liver-cancer-overweight-punch-gut
|passage=Mostly, the microbiome is beneficial. It '''helps''' with digestion and enables people to extract a lot more calories from their food than would otherwise be possible. Research over the past few years, however, has implicated it in diseases from atherosclerosis to asthma to autism.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[avoid]]; to [[prevent]]; to [[refrain]] from; to [[restrain]] (oneself). Usually used in nonassertive contexts with ''[[can]]''.
#: {{ux|en|We couldn’t '''help''' noticing that you were late.}}
#: {{ux|en|We couldn’t '''help''' but notice that you were late.}}
#: {{ux|en|She’s trying not to smile, but she can’t '''help''' herself.}}
#: {{ux|en|Can I '''help''' it if I'm so beautiful?}}
#: {{ux|en|Can I '''help''' it that I fell in love with you?}}
#: {{ux|en|Are they going to beat us?&nbsp;Not if I can '''''help''''' it!}}
#: {{ux|en|She never does more than she can '''help'''.}}
# {{lb|en|Hong Kong|Singapore}} To do something on the [[behalf]] of someone.<ref>{{cite-book|year=2012|chapter=7|author=Tony T.N. Hung|editors=Ee-Ling Low, Azirah Hashim|publisher=John Benjamins Publishing Company|title=English in Southeast Asia: Features, policy and language in use|text=In HKE, the implication is usually to do something on behalf of someone, and not just to assist someone in doing it. For example, when someone asks you “Can you help me wash the dishes?”, the expectation is that you will do it for (rather than with) that person.|page=126}}</ref>
#: {{ux|en|Can you '''help''' me buy some groceries?|footer=Underlying meaning is “Can you go do the groceries for me?”}}
# {{lb|en|Singapore|Singlish|imperative}} To [[assist]] or [[sympathize]] with (the speaker); {{n-g|used to express [[displeasure]], [[disappointment]] or [[exasperation]].}}
#: <small>Compare {{ncog|ms|tolong|t=help; please}} and {{ncog|nan-hbl|導郎|tr=tō͘-lông|t=help; please}}</small>
#: {{co|en|'''Help''' {{l|en|lah|id=attitude}}.|Give me a break.|inline=1}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Use 4 is often used in the [[imperative mood]] as a call for assistance.
* In uses 1, 2, 3 and 4, this is a [[catenative verb]] that takes the ''to infinitive''. It can also take the bare infinitive with no change in meaning.
* In use 5, [[can't help]] is a [[catenative verb]] that takes the ''gerund (-ing)'' or, with but, the bare infinitive.
* For more information, see [[Appendix:English catenative verbs]]

