pyramid.content:
{{also|Pyramid}}
==English==
{{wp}}
[[Image:All Gizah Pyramids.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Great Pyramids of Giza.]]
[[Image:Pyramid (solitaire).jpg|thumb|right|250px|The initial layout of the game of '''Pyramid'''.]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|piramis|pyramis||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fr|pyramide}}, from {{der|en|fro|piramide}}, from {{der|en|la|pyramis|pȳramis, pȳramidis}}, from {{der|en|grc|πῡραμίς}}, possibly from {{m|grc|πῡρός||wheat}} + {{m|grc|ἀμάω||reap}} or from {{der|en|egy|pr-m-ws||height of a pyramid}}, from {{m|egy|prj|pr|(one that) comes forth}} + {{m|egy|m||from}} + {{m|egy|ws||height}}. Schenkel and K. Lang proposed hypothetical {{cog|cop|*ⲡⲓⲣⲁⲙ}} or {{m|cop|*ⲫⲣⲁⲙ}} derived from {{cog|egy|mr}} via metathesis as a source of {{m|grc|πῡραμίς}} while Schenkel also suggested it being the source of {{cog|ar|هَرَم}} although the latter is considered far-fetched by Takacs.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɪɹ.ə.mɪd/|a=RP,GA,CA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-pyramid.ogg|a=California}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɪɹ.ə.məd/|a=AU,NZ,weak vowel merger}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|Q12516}}An ancient massive construction with a square or rectangular base and four triangular sides meeting in an [[apex]], such as those built as tombs in [[Egypt]] or as bases for temples in [[Mesoamerica]].
# A construction in the shape of a pyramid, usually with a square or rectangular base.
#* {{RQ:Telegraph|title=Doddington's garden pyramid is a folly good show: The owners of a Lincolnshire stately home have brought the folly into the 21st century, by building a 30ft pyramid &#91;print edition: Great pyramid of Lincolnshire, 6 September 2014, p.&nbsp;G2&#93;|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/gardening/gardenstovisit/11074319/Doddingtons-garden-pyramid-is-a-folly-good-show.html|date=7 September 2014|passage=[T]he owners of Doddington Hall, in Lincolnshire, have brought the folly into the 21st century, by building a 30ft '''pyramid''' in the grounds of the Elizabethan manor.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250722234638/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/gardening/gardenstovisit/11074319/Doddingtons-garden-pyramid-is-a-folly-good-show.html|archivedate=22 July 2025}}
# {{senseid|en|Q3358290}}{{lb|en|geometry}} A [[solid]] with [[triangular]] [[lateral]] [[face]]s and a [[polygonal]] (often [[square]] or [[rectangular]]) [[base]].
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Any structure or diagram with many members at the bottom and progressively fewer towards the top.
#: {{ux|en|The company was organized as a '''pyramid''', with a CEO in charge of four directors, each heading up a department.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Updike|title='[[w:Rabbit, Run|Rabbit, Run]]'|year=1960| page=63|passage=They sit looking at the empty plate that had held a '''pyramid''' of sesame cakes. They have eaten them all.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=18 Aug 73|volume=1|number=9|journal=Gay Community News|page=1|title=Gay [drawing of a pyramid (sense 1)]|text=A telephone '''pyramid''' is now being formed to fill this need. Once a representative group of community people decide a crisis exists {{...}} a list of people will be called, who in turn will call a small number of others from a list already in their possession. This process continues untill as much of the community as possible has been notified. The numbers will be kept in strict confidence, with no one other than the organizers having the entire list. Individual callers will only have a few names and numbers.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/c5yd9prgvnmo|work=BBC|author=Flora Snelson|title=Cureton, 50, scores in 10th tier to 'complete football'|passage=From there he headed down the leagues, but his love of the game trumped any pride about where he 'ought' to be playing as he racked up more than 1,000 games across the full length of the English '''pyramid'''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251104025436/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/c5yd9prgvnmo|archivedate=4 November 2025}}
# {{lb|en|neuroanatomy}} A [[medullary]] pyramid, the medial-most bumps on the ventral side of the [[medulla oblongata]]
# {{lb|en|UK|dated}} The game of [[pool]] in which the balls are placed in the form of a [[triangle]] at spot.
# A [[pyramid scheme]].
# {{lb|en|card games|uncountable}} {{alternative case form of|en|Pyramid}}. {{gloss|a solitaire card game}}
# {{lb|en|card games}} The [[triangular]] layout of [[card]]s in the game of [[Pyramid]].
#: {{ux|en|Build your '''pyramid''' with all cards face down, except the cards in the bottom row.}}
# {{lb|en|journalism}} An approximately [[triangular]] [[headline]] consisting of several centered lines of text of increasing length.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1924|author=Helen Ogden Mahin|title=The Development and Significance of the Newspaper Headline|passage={{...}} with a cross-line banner, a set of two-column '''pyramids''' beneath it in the middle, and on each side of these exactly the same thing,&mdash;something between a headline and a story&mdash;"$50,000 Reward for&mdash;" etc.}}

