===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The break in the working week, usually two days including the traditional holy or [[sabbath]] day. Thus in Western countries, [[Saturday]] and [[Sunday]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=w:The Gentleman's Magazine|year=1874|month=July–December|title=With the Herring Fleet|author=W. Senior|page=704|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/gentlemansmagaz378unkngoog/page/n718/mode/1up|passage=“They can live upon barley-meal without a morsel of meat from '''week-end''' to '''week-end''', can these miserable Sawnies,” quoth another.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1903|author=Francis Markham|coauthors=Sir Clements Robert Markham|title=Recollections of a town boy at Westminster, 1849–1855|page=34|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/recollectionsoft00markrich/page/34/mode/1up|passage={{...|In former days there was a house at Chiswick occupied as a country residence by the Headmaster, who}} often took a few boys down there for what we North Country folk call the '''week-end''' — Saturday and Sunday; it was also used as a sanatorium if required.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=June 21, 1921|year_published=1928|oclc=499252263|chapter=XX|publisher=Cassell and Company|title=Memories and Reflections 1852–1927|author=[[w:H. H. Asquith|The Earl of Oxford and Asquith, K.G.]]|volume=2|page=197|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.77242/page/n213/mode/1up|passage=I love a phrase of Dizzy's in one of his later letters to Lady Bradford, whom he reproaches for her addiction to what we now call '''week-end''' visits to country houses: “the monotony of organized platitude.”}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|title=Explain it to me quickly: What is a TikTok ‘girl dinner’ and should I eat one tonight?|author=Alyx Gorman; Yvonne C Lam|passage=Yes. Though based on my thorough sampling of the trend, cold pork pies are tragically absent from girl dinner plates. Perhaps because, as noted by many TikTok users – and over the '''weekend''' both Today and the New York Times – many of these girl dinners are “suspiciously low cal”.|url=https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/jul/10/explain-it-to-me-quickly-what-is-a-tiktok-girl-dinner-and-should-i-eat-one-tonight|date=July 10, 2023|accessdate=July 10, 2023|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230801004146/https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/jul/10/explain-it-to-me-quickly-what-is-a-tiktok-girl-dinner-and-should-i-eat-one-tonight}}

====Usage notes====
* Historically in North America and parts of Europe, people would often work on Saturday as well, or at least until noon on Saturday. Thus the “weekend” might begin at noon or later on Saturday in older texts.

To describe the soonest upcoming weekend:
* {{lb|en|UK|New Zealand}} “at the weekend”, “on the weekend”, “this weekend”, “for the weekend”
** {{quote-journal|en
|year=1886
|author=New Zealand Parliament
|title=Parliamentary debates
|volume=324
|page=2371
|passage=Let them work at their ordinary jobs during the week, and then take them out of circulation '''at the weekend''', which is usually the time when the trouble is ...}}
** {{quote-journal|en
|year=2009
|author=Great Britain House of Commons: Business and Enterprise Committee
|title=Pre-appointment Hearing with the Chairman-elect of Ofcom, Dr. Colette Bowe
|page=16
|passage=Whether it is on the BBC, ITV or commercial radio does not really matter. ...can give you a radio example of two things I was listening to '''at the weekend'''.}}
* {{lb|en|Australia|US|Canada}} “on the weekend”, “this weekend”, “for the weekend” (“at the weekend” is not used)
** {{quote-book|en
|year=2002
|author=United States Senate: Committee on Armed Services
|title=Department of Defense authorization for appropriations for fiscal year 2002
|page=722
|passage=I am going to Moscow '''on the weekend''' to participate in the discussion, ...}}

