on foot.content:
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|on fote}}, from {{inh|en|ang|on fōte|t=on foot}}, equivalent to {{af|en|on|foot|nocat=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-on foot.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Prepositional phrase===
{{en-prepphr}}

# On one's feet; [[walking]], [[jogging]] or [[running]] but not in a [[vehicle]] or on the [[back]] of an [[animal]]. {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|on the hoof<qq:idiomatic>}}
#: {{hypo|en|on the run}}
#: {{ux|en|The pub's not far: let's go '''on foot'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1949|month=November and December|title=Notes and News: Festiniog and Welsh Highland Railways|journal=Railway Magazine|page=408|text=At the same time, the encroachment of vegetation proceeds apace, and broom and brambles have already made portions of the line impassable, even '''on foot'''.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare|chiefly|hunting}} [[on the move|On the move]]; [[astir]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{hypo|en|on the run}}
# [[in progress|In progress]] or in [[preparation]]; [[active]], [[in operation]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1789|author={{w|John Moore (Scottish physician)|John Moore}}|title=Zeluco|publisher=Valancourt|year_published=2008|page=113
|passage=[W]ithin a short time it was generally believed at Naples, that a treaty of marriage was '''on foot''' between Zeluco and the young lady in question {{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1902|author=Sir Arthur Conan Doyle|title=The Hound of the Baskervilles|page=122|passage=It had struck me that it was possible that some love intrigue was '''on foot'''. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1921|author={{w|DH Lawrence}}|title=Women in Love|publisher=Vintage|year_published=2008|page=250
|passage=‘I didn't know anything of this sort was '''on foot'''.’ Brangwen smiled awkwardly.}}

